# reaction-gate-runs.log — #215 pre-registration audit trail (rule R4/D).
# Appended by protocol: trigger checks, run stages, artifact hashes, deviations.
# Hashes + counts only — never corpus content.

── D1 (2026-08-02) — read-path environment precondition added to the harness ──
CLASS: mechanics repair. Changes NO threshold, margin, floor, cap, seed, window, or
decision mapping. Computes no quantity differently. Logged BEFORE any in-frame
quantity has been computed (phase 2 has not run; the trigger has not fired).

WHAT CHANGED: reaction-gate-eval.mjs gains verifyReadPathEnv(), asserted in the CLI
immediately after H1 and fail-closed on the same exit(2) path. It refuses to run when
UM_TEMPORAL_QUERY === 'true' in the harness process environment.

WHY THE FROZEN TEXT IS BROKEN (not merely inconvenient): H3(b) requires stratified
read-path probes that call doSearch with the fact's own text and demands rank <= 3 with
at most 1 miss per stratum. That hypothesis is stated in terms of doSearch's ranking
behavior as it existed at freeze (f49e62f), when no read-path window feature existed.
The temporal query-resolution v1 arc introduces UM_TEMPORAL_QUERY, which when 'true'
widens the engine fetch and re-ranks results. Work-context fact text routinely contains
"last week", "in March", "since <date>", "today" — so a non-trivial share of H3 probes
would parse to a window and be re-ranked. The probes read the flag from the HARNESS
process, not the server container, so the deployment-side discipline ("never enable it
on the Pi") does not cover them: an operator running the harness with the flag set in a
local .env (the documented `node --env-file=.env` pattern) would silently perturb H3.
The frozen rule cannot detect this — it has no hypothesis over the harness's own
environment, because the variable did not exist when it was written. That is a genuine
post-freeze gap in the frozen text, not an inconvenience: without the guard the failure
mode is SILENT and would corrupt an un-repeatable measurement.

WHY A GUARD RATHER THAN A RULE CHANGE: the accept rule is untouched and must remain so.
This adds a refusal, not an adjustment — it prevents computing under a condition the
rule implicitly assumed, and can only ever cause the harness to decline to run. It
cannot move any quantity in either direction, so it cannot make a gate easier or harder.

MIRRORED IN SPEC: docs/plans/2026-07-31-reaction-gate-measurement-spec.md (deviations).
ORIGIN: temporal v1 spec section 5 C1; surfaced by the paired-Opus review of that arc.
